                <pb facs="0331" n="227" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg227"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.04/3960: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Consul General at Nanking (Peck)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-02-05T17:00:00-05:00">February 5, 1932—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>21. For the Minister. Your February 4, 5 p.m. In view of Japanese Government’s
                    attitude toward peace proposals and apparent further preparation for hostilities
                    and report now confirmed of despatch of large army contingents to an unknown
                    destination in China, Department does not deem it advisable to make at this time
                    special representations concerning disposal of Japanese naval forces at Nanking
                    to which you refer. However, Department desires to be informed promptly of
                    reaction of British and French Governments, when reported to you by your
                    colleagues, to the suggestion.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
